WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.300
The streaming chat backend.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.300
This is the request path of nearly every LLM product, drawn once properly.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.033
Two rules shape the design.

4
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:12.267
Everything on the hot path is async.

5
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:17.333
Anything slow that is not the LLM call itself moves to background workers.

6
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:19.800
Follow one message.

7
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.333
The client sends it to an async API.

8
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:27.000
State, like history, lives outside the app, so the app scales sideways.

9
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.133
The API calls the LLM.

10
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:32.500
And tokens stream straight back to the client as they arrive.

11
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.567
Now what stays off the hot path.

12
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:38.700
The API does only fast, non-blocking work.

13
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.833
A queue absorbs everything slow.

14
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:44.767
And background workers handle summaries, logging and analytics.

15
00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:51.267
The classic bug: one blocking call inside an async route freezes every user's stream.

16
00:00:51.567 --> 00:01:00.433
The fix: use async clients, push heavy work to workers, and cap concurrent LLM calls with a semaphore, so spikes degrade gracefully.

17
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:02.733
Which transport?

18
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:05.133
SSE suits one-way token flow.

19
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:09.033
WebSocket suits typing indicators and multi-party chat.

20
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:11.800
For plain token streaming, pick SSE.

21
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.467
Now a long conversation.

22
00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:17.533
History grows and the prompt gets slow and costly.

23
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:21.900
Summarize the oldest turns and keep a sliding window of recent ones.

24
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.086
And decide where the summary lives.

25
00:01:24.086 --> 00:01:25.433
Store it outside the app.

26
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:27.407
Let's recap.

27
00:01:27.407 --> 00:01:29.600
The async hot path streams tokens.

28
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.600
Queues absorb everything slow.

29
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.533
State lives outside the app.

30
00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:36.367
And never block inside an async route.

31
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:39.633
Next up: the enterprise multi-tenant AI platform.
